   "source": [
    "# Wine Quality Predictor the Analysis Notebook\n",
    "\n",
    "**PGE 2 · Machine Learning × Advanced Python**\n",
    "\n",
    "We predict whether a Portuguese Vinho Verde wine is good (quality ≥ 6) from its 11 physicochemical measurements. We train a binary classifier on 5,320 wines and compare Logistic Regression, k-NN and Linear SVM under 5-fold cross-validation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "metadata": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": [
    "## Framing\n",
    "\n",
    "**Who uses the tool?**\n",
    "\n",
    "A wine merchant who must choose among hundreds of bottles which ones to send to a panel of expert tasters.\n",
    "\n",
    "**What decision?**\n",
    "\n",
    "The model predicts from chemical measurements whether a wine is good (quality ≥ 6). Wines predicted \"good\" are going to be tasted first.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Cost of a false positive (FP)**\n",
    "\n",
    "The model says good but the wine is not then the panel wastes time on a bad wine and the cost is moderate.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Cost of a false negative**\n",
    "\n",
    "The model says not good but the wine is good: a good wine is never tasted and a sale is lost so we think the cost higher in this use case.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Consequence**\n",
    "\n",
    "We want to avoid FN more than FP because missing a good wine costs more than wasting tasting time. So we prioritize recall on good wines. We track F1 (balance of precision and recall) as our main metric, and the confusion matrix to see exactly how many FP and FN the model makes.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Data limits**\n",
    "\n",
    "The dataset only contains Portuguese Vinho Verde wines rated by human tasters. The model may not generalize to wines from other regions or grape varieties. Quality scores also reflect one panel’s taste, not an objective measure."
   ],
   "id": "e25858d4f27e39c"
  },
  {
